{"reply":"Your reading has a strong philosophical parallel in Plato’s *Meno*: Socratic questioning can leave someone in perplexity, not by supplying an answer but by exposing that their usual categories no longer work.

In Chapter 5, the Caterpillar opens with “Who are _you?_” and, after Alice tries to explain her changing identity, returns to that same question. His calmness makes the exchange feel like an examination, but unlike Socrates, he offers little sign that the confusion will become a shared inquiry. Plato’s Meno gives a useful contrast: Meno describes being reduced to “utter perplexity,” while Socrates says he is still willing to examine the question together ([*Meno*, 80a–d](http://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus%3Atext%3A1999.01.0178%3Atext%3DMeno%3Apage%3D80)).

